---
import DocsPage from "../../layouts/DocsPage.astro"
import { documentCommandRows, knowledgeEval } from "../../data/docs-content"

export const prerender = true
---

<DocsPage id="documents">

          <p>
            From desktop 1.99.110, you can give Vector a repository's specs, PDFs, runbooks and API descriptions once.
            It reads each one on this computer into sections, passages and typed records (requirements, constraints,
            decisions, contracts, procedures, terms, entities, facts and open questions), links them to the code they
            name, and brings the few that bear on a request into it as short cards, inside the budgets described in
            <a href="/docs/brain#requests">Vector Brain</a>.
          </p>

          <h2 id="adding">Adding documents</h2>
          <dl class="doc-rows">
            <div><dt>Settings → Memory → Documents</dt><dd>Drop files there, or add them from your computer: PDF, Word, PowerPoint, Excel, Markdown, HTML, OpenAPI, JSON, YAML, CSV and text, up to 64 MB each (CSV up to 32 MB and text up to 10 MB), and the first 1,000 pages of a PDF. A repository keeps up to 200 documents.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>In the composer</dt><dd>On a document you attach, <strong>Remember</strong> keeps it and still sends it, and <strong>Remember instead</strong> keeps it in place of sending it, so the model gets its outline and the cards for each request instead of every page. Vector suggests Remember instead for a file over 1 MB or a PDF over 20 pages. Word, PowerPoint and Excel files, which models cannot read as they are, are always remembered instead, with Undo. Pasted prose of 4,000 characters or more gets a <strong>Remember this text</strong> chip.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>From the agent</dt><dd>The agent can keep a file it read. The session shows it as Kept, with Undo; after outside content it waits for your review, and a session a guest is in keeps none.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>A new version</dt><dd><strong>Replace</strong> reads a file with the name of a document Vector keeps as that document's next version. Vector says what changed, and keeps your edits and pins.</dd></div>
          </dl>
          <p>
            Pictures of text, such as scanned pages, are named but not read, password-protected parts are left out,
            links to outside files are not followed, and secrets are removed before anything is saved. A passage that
            reads like instructions to an AI, or hides text or characters from a reader, waits for your review. Vector
            does not keep a copy of the file you added unless you turn on <strong>Keep copies of added files</strong>.
          </p>

          <h2 id="privacy">Who a document reaches</h2>
          <p>
            Documents you add are private: kept from invited guests and from models that may train on prompts, and so
            is anything Vector learns from them. A document kept in a session that took in outside content, such as
            web pages or MCP tools, waits for your review before Vector uses it.
          </p>
          <p>
            In repositories you trust, Vector also reads the repository's own READMEs, docs folder, decision records and
            API specs. Those are part of the repository, so they are shared like its other files, also with guests. Turn
            them off with <strong>Read this repository's docs</strong> in Settings → Memory → Documents, which forgets
            what Vector read of them.
          </p>

          <h2 id="code-links">Code links</h2>
          <p>
            Vector links each record to the code that defines, uses or serves what it names: definitions, uses,
            routes, environment variables, constants and file paths in the working tree. A name the code does not have
            yet waits until it does. In a git working tree with documents, Vector also indexes the code's files,
            symbols and imports, and which files change together in its history. It never reads secrets, dependencies
            or build output.
          </p>
          <p>
            Recall starts from the files a request touches and follows those links, so a requirement comes in when you
            open its code, or a file that imports it, even when the request shares no word with it. Search also knows
            the repository's own vocabulary: glossary and acronym records, so "SLO" finds the service level objective.
          </p>
          <ul class="feature-list">
            <li><strong>On a read</strong><span>Reading a linked file adds one short line naming the documents linked to it.</span></li>
            <li><strong>On an edit</strong><span>Editing a file linked to a requirement adds one line asking the agent to check the change still meets it.</span></li>
            <li><strong>Once</strong><span>Each line comes once per file per request, and never for Chat.</span></li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            A document's <strong>Code links</strong> view lists every link, how it was made and whether the code still
            says the same; <strong>Unlink</strong> removes one, and <strong>Link to file…</strong> adds your own.
          </p>

          <h2 id="meaning">Search by meaning</h2>
          <p>
            With the optional local search model, a one-time download you ask for, Vector also finds the records and
            passages that say what a request means in other words, on this computer. Until you download it, documents
            are searched by keywords and code links. See <a href="/docs/search-by-meaning">Search by meaning</a>.
          </p>

          <h2 id="needs-a-look">Drift and conflicts</h2>
          <p>
            When linked code and a document stop agreeing, Vector shows both sides, such as "spec says 50 MB; code says
            64 MB", with the line in the code. It also finds documents, rules and memory that disagree with each other.
            Both go to <strong>Needs a look</strong> in Settings → Memory → Documents, and only you settle them: keep the
            document, keep the code, keep one side, or dismiss it.
          </p>

          <h2 id="citations">Citations</h2>
          <p>
            Every card carries an id. When an answer cites one, it shows as a chip naming the kind of record, the
            document and the page, and why Vector gave it; a chip counts as verified only when Vector gave that card to
            the model in the session. The turn says how many
            tokens of memory it used, and a document's <strong>History</strong> lists its versions and the runs that
            used it.
          </p>

          <h2 id="terminal">In the terminal</h2>
          <p>
            <code>vector memory doc</code> comes with the next CLI release, like <a href="/docs/brain#terminal">the rest
            of <code>vector memory</code></a>.
          </p>
          <dl class="doc-rows">
            {documentCommandRows.map(([term, detail]) => (<div><dt><code>{term}</code></dt><dd>{detail}</dd></div>))}
          </dl>

          <h2 id="measured">What the eval measured</h2>
          <p>
            {knowledgeEval.summary}
          </p>
          <p class="note">{knowledgeEval.caveat}</p>
</DocsPage>
